---
/**
 * What GitHub Pages serves for an address that matches no file.
 *
 * There was no such page for a long time, which meant every mistyped or stale
 * URL landed on the host's own default: a grey page with no sidebar, no search
 * and no way back into the guide. The addresses the guide itself abandoned are
 * handled a level up, by the redirect table in `legacy-urls.mjs`, so anything
 * that reaches this page is a typo, a truncated link, or a page that never
 * existed. All three want the same thing, which is a route back in.
 *
 * The filename matters: Astro emits `src/pages/404.astro` as `dist/404.html`
 * rather than `dist/404/index.html`, and GitHub Pages looks for exactly that.
 */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { guideHref } from "../nav";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<Base
  title="Not found"
  seoTitle="Page not found"
  description="There is no page at this address. The guide index lists every chapter."
  crumbs={[{ label: "Not found" }]}
  noindex
>
  <article>
    <h1>Not found</h1>

    <p class="lede">
      There is no page at this address. Nothing was lost: the guide's own old
      addresses still work and land on the chapter that replaced them, so a
      link that ends up here was either mistyped or cut short somewhere.
    </p>

    <p>
      <a href={guideHref}>The guide index</a> lists every track, section and
      chapter in reading order, which is the fastest way to find what you were
      after. If you know roughly what you want but not where it lives, the
      search box in the header matches every chapter title and heading; press
      <kbd>/</kbd> to open it.
    </p>

    <p>
      If you are starting rather than looking for one page,{" "}
      <a href={`${base}paths/`}>the reading paths</a> lay out five routes
      through the guide depending on where you are coming from: another
      systems language, a scripting language, no systems experience at all.
    </p>

    <p>
      A link on this site that brought you here is a bug worth reporting, and
      it should not be possible: every internal link on every page is resolved
      in a browser before a build is allowed to publish. Either way,{" "}
      <a href={`${base}contact/`}>say where you came from</a> and it gets
      fixed.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
